# Line endings are decided HERE, not by whatever core.autocrlf each machine
# happens to have set.
#
# Without this file the answer depends on the contributor's git config, and
# the symptom is a diff full of whole-file changes that contain no changes.
# That already cost one round of "did the convention change?" -- it had not,
# because core.autocrlf=true was normalising to LF on commit all along, so
# there was never a CRLF convention to violate. This makes that explicit
# rather than incidental.
#
# LF everywhere, including on Windows: every tool in this project reads it
# (PowerShell 5.1, arm-none-eabi-gcc, mingw32-make, Ozone), and one rule is
# easier to keep true than a list of exceptions.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch. Most build outputs are .gitignore'd, but
# these extensions get listed anyway so an accidentally committed one is not
# silently corrupted by line-ending translation.
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.elf   binary
*.bin   binary
*.hex   binary
*.map   binary
*.o     binary
*.so    binary
*.mdl   binary
